<p>A group of independent, UN-appointed human rights experts says allegations described in newly released US Justice Department documents about Jeffrey Epstein’s sex-trafficking network may meet the legal threshold for “crimes against humanity,” pointing to the alleged scale and severity of abuses against women and girls. The experts urged authorities to back an independent investigation based on the newly released material.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/GtVLGFI_UFUd_WcqVrVSpSVTAXKGNloQ9IpW5deCoLx3WJDDzuJwzlDQcfn-60JoCAhtLMxujRKEsvAtgJaWqebILz3JNiNOvwnp1GWkNy368eHYLrIuR2dTVLfbPJ_FlBCUWDzWrkwjLjtDfFWEbkV9iZ9ipJoJFBxyCK_W0Pyr1IxU24ZkYeY4cEvv5R8JUGheSYMShAxe3Z4E5Dmhqtog5EoAl1D9-5COYITDej-k5vWCTc8Hn98WFEX9_wWm4t5t0w' alt='' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Scale described: The UN Human Rights Council-appointed experts pointed to “millions of files,” saying they suggest a “global criminal enterprise”.</li><li>How they framed it: They called the allegations “grave,” citing the reported scale of abuses against women and girls.</li><li>Next step: They urged an independent investigation after the Justice Department document release cited in reports.</li><li>Wider context alleged: The experts linked the alleged crimes to an environment of “supremacist beliefs, racism, corruption and extreme misogyny”.</li><li>Impact on victims: They said the allegations point to the “commodification and dehumanisation of women and girls”.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>UN-appointed independent experts: They said the alleged acts happened against a backdrop of “supremacist beliefs, racism, corruption and extreme misogyny,” and that the crimes described amount to a commodification and dehumanisation of women and girls. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/18/epstein-files-crimes-against-humanity-un-experts'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/18/un-panel-says-epstein-abuses-may-constitute-crimes